# Self-hosting

Wyrd ships as a single server binary. It owns the durable behavior: registry writes, storage orchestration, auth, policy, audit, and the OLAP warehouse. This section is for the person running that binary.

Everything here is operational. If you want the model behind identity, tokens, and roles, read [Concepts](/concepts/). This section tells you which env vars to set, what Postgres and object storage the server needs, and what happens between process start and a server accepting requests.

## Start here

The fastest way to see the server run is local development. Leave the database variables unset and it provisions its own Postgres. Skip the signing key and it mints an ephemeral one. You only have to point it at a storage directory.

```bash
export WYRD_STORAGE_BACKEND=local
export WYRD_STORAGE_LOCAL_ROOT="$PWD/.wyrd-dev/storage"
export WYRD_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8080
mkdir -p "$WYRD_STORAGE_LOCAL_ROOT"

cargo run -p wyrd-server
```

That configuration is development-only. It is not safe for staging or production, and the server refuses to boot a production profile without a real signing key. See [Local development](/self-hosting/local-development/) for the walkthrough, then [Configuration](/self-hosting/configuration/) for the full env var surface a real deployment needs.

